package play

// The tabbed utilities rail, and the D16 default it carries.
//
// # Why the tabs are `ui.Tabs` and the rule is a wrapper attribute
//
// `ui.Tabs` is the shared tabs primitive and it already renders the whole
// pattern: `role="tablist"` with a name, roving `tabindex`, one selected tab,
// `aria-controls` pointing at panels that exist, and `hidden` on the panels that
// are not selected. Re-rendering any of that here would be a second answer to
// the tabs pattern, which is the thing that package exists to prevent.
//
// What `ui.Tabs` has no room for is D16's rule, because the rule is not a
// property of the tabs — it is a property of the *tiers*, and §3.7 resolves tiers
// in the browser. So the rule is carried on the wrapper this component renders
// around it, as `data-tab-defaults`: a JSON object mapping a tab id to the tiers
// at which that tab is the default.
//
// Three things follow from putting it there rather than in the client, and each
// is the reason:
//
//   - **The client knows no tier names.** It reads `data-ui`, looks the tier up
//     in the document's own table, and selects the tab it names. A tier added to
//     the resolver and not to the table cannot reach the document as a wrong
//     answer; it reaches it as a missing key, which
//     `TestTheRuleIsCarriedWholeAndNoTierIsResolved` fails on.
//   - **The rule has a Go test.** `TestTheTierRulePartitionsEveryTierTheResolverCanWrite`
//     asserts every tier is in exactly one tab's list. Ambiguity and absence are
//     both failures, and neither is visible in a browser.
//   - **The document carries every answer.** Seven tiers in, one tab chosen out —
//     on the server that would be a document varying by device, which §13.5 and
//     ADR 0035 rule out. Carrying the whole table makes the document's
//     independence a property of its bytes rather than a promise.
//
// # The server selects one tab, and it is `Tokens`
//
// `ui.Tabs` needs a selection to render, and whatever it renders is what a reader
// with no `data-ui` (no script, or a script that has not run) sees. That reader
// has no tier, so this is the *fourth* case D16 does not name, and it is chosen on
// the merits: on the five tablet tiers the token list is the only representation
// of the table (§4.9) or the primary one (§7.6), and on the three laptop tiers it
// is the accessibility source of truth. Initiative is the better default for a
// GM with a pointer, and it is one `Tab`-and-arrow away from every tier.
//
// **One selection, in every document, on every device.** That is the whole claim,
// and `TestTheRailSelectsTokensWhateverTheCallerAsks` holds the half of it that
// is a server-side decision.

import (
	"github.com/semiplane/semiplane/internal/web/components/ui"
)

// Rail is the tabbed rail: the rule, the campaign it is remembered for, and the
// panels.
//
// The wrapper carries `data-rail-tabs`, which is what the client binds to, and
// `data-campaign`, which keys the remembered tab. Both are needed before the
// first panel exists, so both are here rather than inside `ui.Tabs`.
templ Rail(view RailView) {
	<div
		class="rail-tabs"
		data-rail-tabs
		data-campaign={ view.Campaign }
		data-tab-defaults={ view.defaults().json() }
		data-testid="play-rail"
	>
		@ui.Tabs(tabsView(view))
	</div>
}

// tabsView converts this package's tab shape into the shared primitive's.
//
// One conversion rather than making `ui.Tab` the route's model, for the reason
// `components/viewmodels.go` splits its layers: `ui` cannot import a route's
// model and a route should not be handing a primitive's struct to its own
// business logic. The three fields are copied by name in one place, so the two
// shapes cannot drift in what they share.
//
// **The selection is `TokensTabID` and is not the view's to choose.** There is no
// `Selected` field on `RailView` on purpose, and the argument is the doc comment
// above: a device-dependent selection cannot be made by a server that cannot see
// the device. `TokensTabID` is also the first tab a caller fills, and
// `TestTheRailSelectsTokensWhateverTheCallerAsks` passes a view whose first tab is
// something else to hold it.
func tabsView(view RailView) ui.TabsView {
	tabs := make([]ui.Tab, 0, len(view.Tabs))

	for _, tab := range view.Tabs {
		tabs = append(tabs, ui.Tab{
			ID:      tab.ID,
			Label:   tab.Label,
			Content: tab.Content,
		})
	}

	return ui.TabsView{
		Label:    view.Label,
		Tabs:     tabs,
		Selected: TokensTabID,
		TestID:   "play-rail-tablist",
	}
}

// TokensTab is the rail panel this package owns, as a tab.
//
// A constructor rather than a struct literal at each call site, because a caller
// that filled the id and not the label — or the label and not the content — has
// built a tab that displays one thing and shows another, and both mistakes
// compile identically.
func TokensTab(content templ.Component) Tab {
	return Tab{ID: TokensTabID, Label: "Tokens", Content: content}
}

// The component list, asserted by a test so that a component added here and never
// mounted cannot be quietly forgotten — the same shape `components/chat` uses.
//
// Not a `var`: the point is the constant, and a slice would be a second thing to
// keep true.
const (
	// RailRendered is `Rail`, which is what the play route's rail mounts.
	RailRendered = "Rail"
	// TokenListRendered is `TokenList`, the panel this work item is about.
	TokenListRendered = "TokenList"
)